      "text": "```python\n\"\"\"\nLeast Recently Used (LRU) Cache Implementation\n\nAn LRU cache is a fixed-size cache that evicts the least recently used items\nwhen the cache is full. This implementation uses a combination of a dictionary\nand a doubly-linked list to achieve O(1) time complexity for both get and put\noperations.\n\nThe doubly-linked list maintains the order of usage:\n- The head of the list represents the most recently used item.\n- The tail of the list represents the least recently used item.\n\nWhen an item is accessed (get) or inserted/updated (put), it is moved to the\nhead of the list. When the cache exceeds its capacity, the item at the tail\n(least recently used) is evicted.\n\nTime Complexity:\n- get(key): O(1) average case\n- put(key, value): O(1) average case\n- Space Complexity: O(capacity)\n\nExample:\n    >>> cache = LRUCache(2)\n    >>> cache.put(1, 1)\n    >>> cache.put(2, 2)\n    >>> cache.get(1)       # returns 1\n    1\n    >>> cache.put(3, ",
